{
  "abstract": "Let a biplanar graph on 24 vertices have a universal vertex and a second vertex whose only nonneighbors are two adjacent exceptions. Delete these four vertices to form the twenty-vertex body. We prove that a graph in this class requiring ten colors must have exactly eight body neighbors at each exception and exactly 132 edges. The proof compares two critical subgraphs on the same body. For an exception with seven neighbors, an independent-set deletion, a rooted partition certificate and a matching on thirteen vertices provide compatible colorings. When the neighborhoods are disjoint, the low-vertex theorem forces a seven-clique in the body, which is incompatible with biplanarity. The non-Ore density theorem of Kostochka and Yancey excludes an exception with nine neighbors for every overlap size. Each augmented body that remains would be a three-connected eight-critical graph on 21 vertices with 80 edges. Its existence would violate Ore's proposed edge recurrence from order 14 to order 21 at chromatic number eight. There are six remaining intersection sizes, and at seven common neighbors the exclusive neighbors are nonadjacent. The supplement gives the full reductions, identifies the published partition-certificate inputs and includes the arithmetic replays.",
